<p>Conferences provide valuable opportunities to academics and can influence disciplinary agendas.<span id="more-56821"></span></p>
<p>But scholars have unequal access to conferences, often owing to where such conferences are held and the availability of funding.</p>
<p>As <a href="https://kritika-maheshwari.carrd.co/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kritika Maheshwari</a> (Delft University of Technology), <a href="https://www.unisg.ch/en/university/about-us/organisation/detail/person-id/5917eced-714b-48d8-85bf-c6276b3685f2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thierry Ngosso</a> (University of St. Gallen), and <a href="https://sites.google.com/site/brianberkey/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brian Berkey</a> (University of Pennsylvania) explain in the this guest post, this is not bad just for those scholars, but &#8220;for philosophy as a discipline.&#8221;</p>
<p>They have each been involved in organizing conferences around the world, and in what follows they discuss why it is worthwhile to do so, and share some advice based on their experiences.</p>
